{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.macro.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# yin.vm.macro — Macro Expansion as a Stream Process\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: agreed design target (2026-09-13). This document is the macro\n     4\tcontract for `yin.vm`. It supersedes the v1 stream model in\n     5\t[`macro-design.md`](./macro-design.md), the strategy in\n     6\t[`../cross-language-macro.md`](../cross-language-macro.md), and the previous\n     7\trevision of this document, which integrated expansion into the evaluators;\n     8\tAppendix A records what that revision proposed and why it is withdrawn. It\n     9\tis subordinate to [`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md) and\n    10\t[`dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md).\n    11\t\n    12\tThe design follows from one framing and adds nothing to it:\n    13\t\n    14\t> Every `yin.vm` evaluator is an observer of a `dao.stream` medium. The\n    15\t> writer to that medium is just another process. Macro expansion is such a\n    16\t> process.\n    17\t\n    18\t---\n    19\t\n    20\t## 0. Decisions\n    21\t\n    22\t1. **Evaluators know nothing about macros.** No evaluator — `ast-walker`,\n    23\t   `linearize`/`semantic`, or a future stack VM — has a macro transition, a\n    24\t   macro instruction, a macro flag on closures, a macro option at\n    25\t   construction, or an expansion ledger. An evaluator executes semantic\n    26\t   datoms and rejects, at load, any node type outside its vocabulary.\n    27\t2. **Expansion is a process between two media.** The expander observes a\n    28\t   *source* medium (`program-in`), rewrites each batch to a fixpoint, and\n    29\t   appends the result to a *program* medium (`program-out`) that evaluators\n    30\t   observe. It is a forwarder in the sense of `dao.stream.md` §Composition,\n    31\t   driven by the same `stream-observer/run-on-stream` coordination that\n    32\t   drives an evaluator.\n    33\t3. **There is no phase.** \"Compile time\" and \"runtime\" named which cursor\n    34\t   reached a batch first. With expansion on its own medium, the distinction\n    35\t   has no referent; `:yin/phase-policy` and `:yin/phase` are retired.\n    36\t4. **A macro call site is an ordinary application.** In datom form, every\n    37\t   application's operands are already unevaluated syntax, so nothing marks a\n    38\t   call site. The expander rewrites an `:application` whose operator\n    39\t   resolves, under lexical shadowing, to a lambda carrying `:yin/macro? true`.\n    40\t   `:yin/macro-expand` is retired from the Universal AST.\n    41\t5. **Definitions never reach an evaluator.** The expander consumes every\n    42\t   macro definition in a batch into its own store and forwards only expanded\n    43\t   program. A macro name reaching an evaluator is therefore an unbound\n    44\t   variable — a loud failure — never a function applied to evaluated\n    45\t   arguments.\n    46\t6. **Macros are closed syntax transformers.** A macro body receives operand\n    47\t   ASTs bound to its parameters and returns a plain-data AST. It sees no\n    48\t   program store, no captured environment, no streams. Expansion is a pure\n    49\t   function of (macro lambda, operand ASTs, allocation state) on every host.\n    50\t7. **Expansion is outermost-first and re-expands its own output to a\n    51\t   fixpoint** under dual guards (depth, output size per expansion; steps per\n    52\t   body; optional cumulative bound per batch) with cycle detection.\n    53\t8. **Output batches are self-contained.** Unchanged operands are copied,\n    54\t   not referenced, into `program-out`; refs across media are meaningful only\n    55\t   for durable ids, and the expander does not pretend otherwise.\n    56\t9. **Provenance is a third medium.** Every attempt produces a\n    57\t   `:macro-expand-event`; events and the `m`-tagged output copies are\n    58\t   appended to an optional *log* medium. Evaluators never see events.\n    59\t10. **Authority is write authority on a medium.** Whoever may append to\n    60\t    `program-in` may define macros; whoever holds the `program-out` writer\n    61\t    decides what programs run. No VM option encodes this.\n    62\t\n    63\t---\n    64\t\n    65\t## 1. Axioms and invariants\n    66\t\n    67\t**Interpretation creates semantics.** A `:lambda` entity with\n    68\t`:yin/macro? true` is an ordinary lambda. An `:application` whose operator\n    69\tnames it is an ordinary application. What makes the pair a macro and a call\n    70\tsite is *which process reads them*: the expander reads them as a rewrite\n    71\trule and a rewrite site; an evaluator, if it ever saw them, would read them\n    72\tas a function and a call. The design keeps them on the expander's side of a\n    73\tmedium boundary, so the evaluator never has to know the difference.\n    74\t\n    75\t**Code and state are datoms.** Definitions, call sites, expansion output,\n    76\tand events are all `:yin/*` and `:macro-expand-event` datoms. Original call\n    77\tdatoms on `program-in` are never rewritten; `program-out` is a new, complete\n    78\tprogram. The link from a call to its expansion is forward-only, through the\n    79\tevent.\n    80\t\n    81\t**Everything is a stream.** The expander is a forwarder: one batch in, one\n    82\tbatch out, `full` retained for retry, cadence owned by the driver. Nothing\n    83\tabout it requires support from the stream contract or from any evaluator.\n    84\tAn evaluator observing `program-out` is exactly the evaluator observing any\n    85\tprogram medium.\n    86\t\n    87\t**Everything is a continuation.** Nothing here touches continuations. A\n    88\tcontinuation captured by an evaluator references only semantic datoms,\n    89\twhich is why a shipped continuation needs no expander at its destination.\n    90\t\n    91\t| Invariant | How the design honours it |\n    92\t|---|---|\n    93\t| No hidden global state | The macro environment is the expander's own store, a value in its process state. Fresh ids and gensyms come from an allocation counter seeded from each batch and returned as a watermark. No registry, no `defonce`. |\n    94\t| No implicit control flow | Expansion happens in exactly one place: the expander's step. Nothing expands on lookup, load, apply, or transition. |\n    95\t| No callbacks | The expander is a function over a batch and a context returning data; its driver is `run-on-stream`. The body runner is a function called synchronously on a throwaway VM value. |\n    96\t| No shared mutable state | Expander state is a persistent map threaded through the step. The one local cell — a gensym counter inside one body invocation — cannot escape because the output validator rejects any host value. |\n    97\t| No layer collapsing | *compile* (yang) → medium → *expand* → medium → *load/execute*. Each is a function over data. The evaluator never re-enters expansion; the expander never holds VM control state. |\n    98\t| No assumed graphs | The expander indexes each batch with `index-datoms` explicitly, visits ref attributes in a fixed order, tracks the descent path for cycles, and honours the explicit `:yin/root` fact. Unresolved names, dangling roots, and forged ids are errors naming the entity. |\n    99\t\n   100\t---\n   101\t\n   102\t## 2. Representation\n   103\t\n   104\t### 2.1 The macro entity\n   105\t\n   106\t| Attribute | Value | Note |\n   107\t|---|---|---|\n   108\t| `:yin/type` | `:lambda` | structurally a lambda |\n   109\t| `:yin/params` | vector of symbols; `&` before the last permitted | variadic binding is the expander's (§3.3) |\n   110\t| `:yin/body` | ref | |\n   111\t| `:yin/macro?` | `true` | the only fact that distinguishes a macro |\n   112\t\n   113\tAST form `{:type :lambda :params [...] :body ... :macro? true}`. `yang.clojure`\n   114\tlowers `(defmacro name [params] body)` — at any nesting — to\n   115\t`(yin/def name <lambda macro? true>)`, exactly as `def` lowers today with the\n   116\tflag added. `^{:yang/shadow-params-operand}` / `^{:yang/shadow-body-start}`\n   117\tmetadata is retired (§3.2 explains why outermost-first expansion makes the\n   118\thint unnecessary).\n   119\t\n   120\t### 2.2 The definition form\n   121\t\n   122\tA **definition** is an `:application` whose operator is\n   123\t`{:type :variable :name yin/def}` and whose operands are\n   124\t`[{:type :literal :value <sym>} <lambda macro? true>]` — the shape\n   125\t`yang.clojure/compile-def` already emits. The expander recognises this shape\n   126\tanywhere in a batch (§3.1).\n   127\t\n   128\t### 2.3 The call site\n   129\t\n   130\tAny `:application` whose operator is a `:variable` naming a macro in scope,\n   131\tor is itself a `:lambda` with `:macro? true`. Operands are whatever the\n   132\tfrontend compiled them to — canonical Universal AST nodes, never\n   133\tsource-language forms. A Python `defn(\"f\", [\"x\"], x)` and a Clojure\n   134\t`(defn f [x] x)` are both `:application`s; the frontend does no discovery and\n   135\tno coercion. **Contract for macro authors:** use the prelude accessors\n   136\t(`yin/name-of`) for names rather than matching on `:type`, because a name\n   137\tmay arrive as a `:variable`, a `:literal` symbol, or a `:literal` string.\n   138\t\n   139\t### 2.4 The root fact and the codec\n   140\t\n   141\t`ast->datoms-with-root` appends `[root :yin/root true t default-op]`;\n   142\t`index-datoms` takes the *last* `:yin/root` fact in batch order as the\n   143\troot, falls back to the existing heuristic when none is present, and fails\n   144\t`{:rule :dangling-root :entity e}` if the named entity has no `:yin/type`. An\n   145\texplicit `:root-id` option still overrides. This is a codec improvement\n   146\tindependent of macros; the expander relies on it because it emits copies\n   147\tbottom-up and must not depend on emission order for root discovery.\n   148\t\n   149\tThe codec (`yin.vm/ast->datoms`, `datoms->ast`) keeps `:yin/macro?` on\n   150\tlambdas and gains `:yin/root`. It drops `:yin/macro-expand`,\n   151\t`:yin/phase-policy`, `:yin/phase`, `:yin/capability` and the\n   152\t`:macro-expand-event` attributes: events are written by the expander through\n   153\tits own small emitter (§4.1), not through the AST codec, because they are\n   154\tnot AST.\n   155\t\n   156\t---\n   157\t\n   158\t## 3. The expander (`yin.vm.macro`)\n   159\t\n   160\t### 3.1 Contract\n   161\t\n   162\t```\n   163\texpand-batch : datoms ctx → {:datoms out  :root-eid r  :events [...]  :log [...]  :ctx ctx'}\n   164\texpand       : datoms ctx → out                       ; = (:datoms (expand-batch ...))\n   165\tdefinitions  : datoms → {sym lambda-ast}              ; syntactic harvest, last wins\n   166\tinvoke       : lambda-ast operand-asts ctx → value     ; one body, §3.3\n   167\tvalid-ast?   : value → nil | {:kind :invalid-output :at path}\n   168\tmark-tail    : ast tail? → ast                         ; §3.4\n   169\tstep         : expander → expander'                    ; one forwarder step, §5\n   170\tstdlib-forms, prelude\n   171\t```\n   172\t\n   173\t`ctx` is the expander's process state and is threaded through every call:\n   174\t\n   175\t```\n   176\t{:store    {sym lambda-ast}       ; macro environment, persisted across batches\n   177\t :alloc    {:next-eid n}          ; monotonic negative counter, seeded per batch\n   178\t :guards   {:max-depth 100  :max-nodes-per-expansion 10000\n   179\t            :max-steps 100000  :max-datoms-per-batch nil}\n   180\t :eval     (fn [lambda-ast env prelude budget] → value)   ; §3.3\n   181\t :t        0}\n   182\t```\n   183\t\n   184\t`expand-batch`:\n   185\t\n   186\t1. `definitions` harvests every definition form (§2.2) in the batch, last in\n   187\t   batch order wins, and merges into `:store`. Harvest is *syntactic*: a\n   188\t   definition in an unexecuted branch is still a definition, as in Clojure.\n   189\t2. Each definition node is replaced by `{:type :literal :value <sym>}`, so a\n   190\t   batch whose root is a definition still forwards a complete program and a\n   191\t   REPL prints the name.\n   192\t3. `expand-node` (§3.2) runs from the root; output is emitted bottom-up as a\n   193\t   self-contained batch with a fresh `:yin/root` fact.\n   194\t4. A final linear scan asserts the output contains no lambda with\n   195\t   `:yin/macro? true` and no application whose operator resolves in\n   196\t   `:store` — fixpoint by construction, checked anyway.\n   197\t\n   198\t`out` is a complete program batch: `datoms->ast` loads it on any evaluator.\n   199\tSame inputs, same output, on every host.\n   200\t\n   201\t**Allocation.** `:alloc` is seeded per batch to\n   202\t`(min (dec min-eid-in-batch) (- (inc datom/first-user-id)))` so every fresh\n   203\tid is a valid tempid even when the batch carries committed positive ids, and\n   204\tis returned advanced.\n   205\t\n   206\t### 3.2 The algorithm\n   207\t\n   208\tTraversal visits ref attributes in a **fixed order** so fresh ids and\n   209\tgensyms are deterministic across hosts: `:yin/operator`, `:yin/operands`\n   210\t(left to right), `:yin/test`, `:yin/consequent`, `:yin/alternate`,\n   211\t`:yin/body`, `:yin/target`, `:yin/val-node`, `:yin/source`. `path` is the\n   212\tset of eids on the current descent; `shadow` is the set of names bound by\n   213\tenclosing `:lambda :params`.\n   214\t\n   215\t```\n   216\texpand-node ctx node path shadow depth :\n   217\t  if eid(node) ∈ path: fail {:kind :cyclic-input :eid}\n   218\t  macro = case operator(node)\n   219\t            :variable v  → v ∉ shadow ∧ (:store ctx) v\n   220\t            :lambda  l   → l when (:macro? l)\n   221\t            _            → nil\n   222\t  if node is :application ∧ macro:\n   223\t      guard depth\n   224\t      ev        = fresh-event ctx                     ; allocated first, so every failure is recorded\n   225\t      operands  = operands(node)                      ; unexpanded AST maps\n   226\t      out       = invoke macro operands ctx           ; §3.3\n   227\t      check valid-ast? out ; node count ≤ :max-nodes-per-expansion\n   228\t      out'      = mark-tail out (:tail? node)         ; §3.4\n   229\t      record event ev node macro out'\n   230\t      return expand-node ctx out' path shadow (depth+1)     ; re-expand the output\n   231\t  else:\n   232\t      shadow' = if node is :lambda then shadow ∪ params(node) else shadow\n   233\t      children' = [expand-node ctx c (path ∪ {eid}) shadow' depth | c ∈ children(node)]\n   234\t      return node with children'\n   235\t```\n   236\t\n   237\tProperties, each a Phase 1 test:\n   238\t\n   239\t- **Outermost first.** A macro sees its operands as written. This is what\n   240\t  makes `->`-style macros expressible and what retires the shadow hints:\n   241\t  in `(defn f [x] (x 1))`, `defn` expands *before* its operands are examined,\n   242\t  yielding `(yin/def f (lambda [x] (x 1)))`; re-expansion then meets `(x 1)`\n   243\t  under `:lambda [x]`, so `x` is shadowed and left alone even if a macro\n   244\t  named `x` exists. yang's frontend hint existed only because the frontend\n   245\t  had to decide this before expansion.\n   246\t- **Fixpoint by construction.** Every node reachable from the final root\n   247\t  was visited after its last rewrite.\n   248\t- **Depth** counts nesting of expansion inside expansion output. Direct\n   249\t  recursion without a base case fails at 100 with\n   250\t  `{:kind :depth-guard :depth 100 :chain [eid ...]}`. Siblings do not\n   251\t  accumulate depth.\n   252\t- **Shadowing is lexical and complete.** `let`, `do`, and every binder every\n   253\t  frontend emits lower to `:lambda :params`; `yin/def` is global and is the\n   254\t  store. There is no other binder in the Universal AST.\n   255\t- **Failure leaves a record.** The attempt event is allocated before\n   256\t  resolution and invocation, so arity, output, guard, and fuel failures are\n   257\t  all written with `:yin/error` (§4.1) before the step throws.\n   258\t\n   259\t### 3.3 Executing the macro lambda\n   260\t\n   261\t`invoke` binds positional params one AST map each and `& rest` to a vector\n   262\tof the remaining maps (arity mismatch is\n   263\t`{:kind :arity :macro eid :expected n :got m}`) and calls\n   264\t`((:eval ctx) lambda-ast env prelude budget)`.\n   265\t\n   266\tThe expander supplies its own body runner; today it is a throwaway\n   267\t`yin.vm.ast-walker` driven by `step` in a counted loop, because\n   268\t`engine/run-loop` has no fuel and a body that loops forever would otherwise\n   269\tstall the forwarder:\n   270\t\n   271\t```\n   272\t(defn run-bounded [lambda-ast env prelude budget]\n   273\t  (let [vm (ast-walker/create-vm {:env env :primitives (merge vm/primitives prelude)})\n   274\t        vm (ast-walker/vm-load-program vm (vm/ast->datoms (:body lambda-ast)))]\n   275\t    (loop [vm vm, n 0]\n   276\t      (cond (> n budget)            (throw (ex-info \"Macro body exceeded step budget\" {:kind :fuel-guard :steps n}))\n   277\t            (vm/blocked? vm)        (throw (ex-info \"Macro body suspended\" {:kind :suspended}))\n   278\t            (seq (:parked vm))      (throw (ex-info \"Macro body parked\" {:kind :suspended}))\n   279\t            (seq (:ready-queue vm)) (throw (ex-info \"Macro body scheduled work\" {:kind :suspended}))\n   280\t            (vm/halted? vm)         (vm/value vm)\n   281\t            :else                   (recur (vm/step vm) (inc n))))))\n   282\t```\n   283\t\n   284\tThis is a private detail of the expander, not a VM feature: the throwaway\n   285\tVM is an ordinary evaluator running an ordinary lambda. It has no\n   286\t`:make-stream` and no FFI pair, so stream and FFI effects fail with the\n   287\texisting constructor errors; `yin/def` inside a body writes to the throwaway\n   288\tstore and is discarded; `require` resolves against `nil` modules and fails.\n   289\tThe body sees only its parameters, the prelude, and `yin.vm/primitives` —\n   290\tdecision 6. Macro authors are told plainly: *a macro body may not call a\n   291\tfunction the program defined; helpers for macros are other macros or\n   292\tprelude calls.*\n   293\t\n   294\t\"Run a lambda under a step budget in a fresh VM\" is the one capability the\n   295\texpander needs that `yin.vm` does not export today. It is a general\n   296\tfacility — any process that runs another's code wants it — and is the\n   297\treason the expander can later become a `yin.vm` program itself (§7,\n   298\tReserved) without any macro-specific primitive.\n   299\t\n   300\t**Output validation.** The body's value must be a closed, plain-data\n   301\tUniversal AST. `valid-ast?` enforces, per node, exactly the vocabulary\n   302\t`ast->datoms-with-root` emits:\n   303\t\n   304\t| `:type` | Required | Optional |\n   305\t|---|---|---|\n   306\t| `:literal` | `:value` plain data | `:tail?` |\n   307\t| `:variable` | `:name` symbol | `:tail?` |\n   308\t| `:lambda` | `:params` vector of symbols, `:body` | `:tail?` `:macro?` |\n   309\t| `:application` | `:operator`, `:operands` vector | `:tail?` |\n   310\t| `:if` | `:test` `:consequent` `:alternate` | `:tail?` |\n   311\t| `:dao.stream.apply/call` | `:op` keyword, `:operands` | `:tail?` |\n   312\t| `:vm/gensym` | | `:prefix` string |\n   313\t| `:vm/store-get` | `:key` | |\n   314\t| `:vm/store-put` | `:key` `:val` plain data | |\n   315\t| `:vm/park`, `:vm/current-continuation` | | |\n   316\t| `:vm/resume` | `:parked-id` keyword, `:val` | |\n   317\t| `:stream/make` | | `:buffer` int |\n   318\t| `:stream/put` | `:target` `:val` | |\n   319\t| `:stream/cursor`, `:stream/next`, `:stream/close` | `:source` | |\n   320\t\n   321\tAny other `:type`, any key outside the row, any `:eid`, or any non-plain\n   322\tvalue anywhere (recursively through vectors, maps, and sets, including\n   323\tinside `:literal :value`) is `{:kind :invalid-output :macro eid :at [path]}`.\n   324\t*Plain data* is nil, booleans, numbers, strings, keywords, symbols, and\n   325\tvectors/maps/sets thereof. `:vm/store-update` is absent: it is a walker-only\n   326\ttransition with no datom form. The walk also counts nodes for the\n   327\tper-expansion bound, checked *before* emission so oversized output never\n   328\tmaterialises.\n   329\t\n   330\t**The prelude** — primitives merged into the throwaway VM for one body:\n   331\t\n   332\t| Primitive | Meaning |\n   333\t|---|---|\n   334\t| `yin/node-type ast`, `yin/node-get ast k` | accessors |\n   335\t| `yin/literal v`, `yin/variable sym` | leaf constructors |\n   336\t| `yin/lambda params body`, `yin/application op operands`, `yin/if t c a` | node constructors |\n   337\t| `yin/sequence-body [ast ...]` | the `do` desugaring yang uses; empty → `nil` literal |\n   338\t| `yin/make-lambda params-ast body-ast` | `params-ast` is a `:literal` whose value is the param vector |\n   339\t| `yin/make-def name-ast value-ast` | `(yin/def <sym> value)`; `name-ast` may be `:variable`, `:literal` symbol, or `:literal` string |\n   340\t| `yin/name-of ast` | the symbol named by any of those three shapes; error otherwise |\n   341\t| `yin/gensym-sym prefix` | fresh symbol `prefix__<E>_<n>`, `E` = absolute event eid, `n` invocation-local |\n   342\t\n   343\tNo constructor marks tail positions (§3.4). A macro that wants to emit a\n   344\tcall to another macro emits an ordinary `yin/application`; re-expansion\n   345\thandles it. **Hygiene** is non-hygienic by default with `yin/gensym-sym` on\n   346\trequest; automatic hygiene is reserved.\n   347\t\n   348\t`stdlib-forms` (the `defn` macro) is recovered from\n   349\t`d8b27a5^:src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc` with its shadow metadata removed:\n   350\t\n   351\t```clojure\n   352\t'[(defmacro defn [fn-name fn-params & body]\n   353\t    (let [b (yin/sequence-body body)\n   354\t          l (yin/make-lambda fn-params b)]\n   355\t      (yin/make-def fn-name l)))]\n   356\t```\n   357\t\n   358\t### 3.4 Tail positions in expansion output\n   359\t\n   360\tTail-ness is contextual, so constructors cannot mark it. `mark-tail ast\n   361\ttail?` is one deterministic pass over each expansion's output before\n   362\tre-expansion:\n   363\t\n   364\t- the root receives the call site's `:tail?`;\n   365\t- `:if` passes its flag to `:consequent` and `:alternate`;\n   366\t- an `:application` in tail position is marked; if its operator is a\n   367\t  `:lambda`, that lambda's body inherits the flag (yang's `let`/`do`\n   368\t  desugaring);\n   369\t- every `:lambda` body is in tail position relative to its own return;\n   370\t- no other node is marked.\n   371\t\n   372\tThe walker never reads `:tail?`; `linearize` and the stack VM do. Without\n   373\tthis pass a tail-position macro expanding to an ordinary application would\n   374\tpush one frame per iteration on the semantic VM while the walker stayed\n   375\tflat.\n   376\t\n   377\t### 3.5 Guards\n   378\t\n   379\t| Guard | Scope | Default | Error |\n   380\t|---|---|---|---|\n   381\t| depth | per expansion chain | 100 | `{:kind :depth-guard :depth :chain}` |\n   382\t| nodes per expansion | one macro's output | 10,000 | `{:kind :datom-guard :scope :expansion :emitted :call}` |\n   383\t| datoms per batch | cumulative over one `expand-batch` | unlimited | `{:kind :datom-guard :scope :batch :emitted}` |\n   384\t| steps | one body invocation | 100,000 | `{:kind :fuel-guard :steps}` |\n   385\t| cyclic input | traversal | — | `{:kind :cyclic-input :eid}` |\n   386\t| arity / output / suspended | — | — | `{:kind :arity}` `{:kind :invalid-output :at}` `{:kind :suspended}` |\n   387\t\n   388\t---\n   389\t\n   390\t## 4. Provenance\n   391\t\n   392\t### 4.1 The expansion event\n   393\t\n   394\tEvery attempt, successful or not, produces one event. Events and the\n   395\t`m`-tagged output are appended to the **log** medium, when the expander has\n   396\tone; `program-out` receives only program.\n   397\t\n   398\t```\n   399\t[ev :yin/type           :macro-expand-event  t default-op]\n   400\t[ev :yin/source-call    <call-eid>           t default-op]   ; eid as written in the source batch\n   401\t[ev :yin/macro-name     <sym>                t default-op]   ; always\n   402\t[ev :yin/macro          <lambda-eid>         t default-op]   ; only when durable (§4.2)\n   403\t[ev :yin/expansion-root <root'>              t default-op]   ; on success\n   404\t[ev :yin/error          {:kind ...}          t default-op]   ; on failure\n   405\t[ev :yin/timestamp      <logical-t>          t default-op]   ; the ctx :t, never a host clock\n   406\t```\n   407\t\n   408\tExpansion output datoms carry `m = ev`; copies of unchanged operands and\n   409\tancestors carry `m = default-op` (re-parenting, not macro output). Query\n   410\tchain: source-call → event → expansion-root; \"every datom this expansion\n   411\tproduced\" is `[?d _ _ _ ?ev]`.\n   412\t\n   413\t### 4.2 Identity across media\n   414\t\n   415\t`program-in`, `program-out`, and the log are different media. A ref written\n   416\ton one that names an entity on another is meaningful only when the named\n   417\tid is **durable** — a permanent positive id from a committed stream. The\n   418\ttransactor (`dao.space.transact`) resolves tempids within one transaction;\n   419\tcommitting two batches separately does not connect them. Therefore:\n   420\t\n   421\t- `program-out` batches are self-contained: unchanged subtrees are copied,\n   422\t  with fresh ids, not referenced (decision 8).\n   423\t- `:yin/source-call` on an event names the call's eid *as it appeared in\n   424\t  the source batch*; it is descriptive until both media are committed under\n   425\t  one resolution. `:yin/macro` is written only when the macro's id is\n   426\t  durable; otherwise `:yin/macro-name` carries the name. Names are\n   427\t  metadata, never identity repair.\n   428\t- A pipeline that wants queryable provenance commits `program-in` first and\n   429\t  expands against durable ids — the ordering rule of\n   430\t  `cross-language-macro.md` §4, retained. `ADR 0003` makes `dao.space` the\n   431\t  medium for that commit.\n   432\t\n   433\t### 4.3 Authority\n   434\t\n   435\tWrite authority on a medium is the whole model. Defining a macro is\n   436\tappending a definition to `program-in`; running a program is appending to\n   437\t`program-out`; both are decided by whoever holds the writer, and\n   438\t`dao.stream.md` puts that decision in the composition. A Shibi\n   439\tcapability, when it exists, is verified at the medium's boundary before the\n   440\tappend, not inside any VM. The old `:macro-authorize` VM option is gone\n   441\tbecause there is nothing left inside a VM to authorize.\n   442\t\n   443\t---\n   444\t\n   445\t## 5. The expander as an observer\n   446\t\n   447\tThe expander is driven by `yin.vm.stream-observer/run-on-stream` with\n   448\tits state in the `:vm` slot — the coordination inspects no evaluator field,\n   449\tso it drives an expander as readily as a VM:\n   450\t\n   451\t```\n   452\texpander = {:ctx ctx  :out writer  :log writer|nil  :staged nil}\n   453\t\n   454\tready?       (fn [x] (nil? (:staged x)))\n   455\tload-program (fn [x batch] (let [r (expand-batch batch (:ctx x))]\n   456\t                             (assoc x :ctx (:ctx r) :staged r)))\n   457\trun-vm       (fn [x] (flush x))       ; append (:datoms staged) to :out and (:log staged) to :log;\n   458\t                                      ; :ok → :staged nil; :full → keep :staged, remain not-ready\n   459\t```\n   460\t\n   461\t`run-on-stream`'s contract does the rest: a throwing `load-program`\n   462\tpropagates before the source cursor advances, so a malformed batch is\n   463\tretried and never half-forwarded; a `full` on `program-out` leaves the\n   464\texpander not-ready with the exact batch staged, so the next round retries\n   465\tthe append before observing again. `gap` on `program-in` is counted by the\n   466\tobserver and is the composition's policy, as for any observer.\n   467\t\n   468\t```\n   469\t  yang ──▶ program-in ──observe──▶ expander ──append──▶ program-out ──observe──▶ evaluator\n   470\t                                       │\n   471\t                                       └──append──▶ log (events, m-tagged output)\n   472\t```\n   473\t\n   474\tEvery evaluator observes `program-out` through its unchanged loader:\n   475\t`ast-walker/vm-load-program`, `linearize/loader ∘ semantic`, the stack\n   476\tcompiler. None composes anything macro-related.\n   477\t\n   478\t---\n   479\t\n   480\t## 6. Compositions\n   481\t\n   482\t### 6.1 `yin.repl`\n   483\t\n   484\t`make-session` builds `program-in`, the expander (with `stdlib-forms`\n   485\tpre-loaded into its store), `program-out`, an optional log medium, and the\n   486\tevaluator's observer on `program-out`. An evaluation round is: append the\n   487\tfrontend's datoms to `program-in`; drive the expander with `run-on-stream`\n   488\tuntil ready; drive the evaluator with `run-on-stream` on `program-out`;\n   489\tdrain output. `eval-ast` takes this same path (`vm/eval` is no longer a\n   490\tprogram path in the shell). `(compile expr)` renders AST, source datoms,\n   491\texpanded datoms, and events. `(reset)` and `(vm ...)` rebuild the whole\n   492\tsession, which clears the expander's store with the evaluator's.\n   493\t\n   494\tThe shell's frontends are untouched: `yang.clojure/compile` and\n   495\t`compile-program` lose their `macro-env` arities; `yang.python` and\n   496\t`yang.php` never had any and gain none.\n   497\t\n   498\t### 6.2 `yang.clojure`\n   499\t\n   500\t`defmacro` stays a special form lowering to `(yin/def name <lambda macro?\n   501\ttrue>)`. Deleted: `initial-macro-env`, `shadow-macro-env`,\n   502\t`compile-macro-operands`, `compile-defn`'s native fallback (the `defn` macro\n   503\tis always in the expander's store), the `macro-env` parameter on every\n   504\t`compile-*`, and `:yin/macro-expand` emission. `compile-program` returns\n   505\tAST only.\n   506\t\n   507\t### 6.3 Non-homoiconic frontends\n   508\t\n   509\t`yang.python` and `yang.php` emit `:application` for every call, as they do\n   510\ttoday. A call to a macro defined in Clojure is expanded because the\n   511\texpander's store has it — cross-language macro use costs the frontend\n   512\tnothing. Authoring macros from Python or PHP is the prelude behind a\n   513\t`defmacro` surface and is reserved; the Python tokenizer has no list-literal\n   514\tproduction, so a Python `defn` call is not yet expressible.\n   515\t\n   516\t### 6.4 Build pipelines\n   517\t\n   518\tA build composes the same forwarder between file-backed media\n   519\t(`dao.stream.file.md`), committing `program-in` to `dao.space` first when it\n   520\twants durable provenance (§4.2). The expander's store can be seeded from a\n   521\tcommitted stream with `definitions`; a DaoDB query for\n   522\t`[?e :yin/macro? true]` joined to its `yin/def` is a future producer of the\n   523\tsame map.\n   524\t\n   525\t### 6.5 Cross-platform\n   526\t\n   527\tOne `.cljc`; the expander is maps, vectors, and `reduce`. Symbols from\n   528\t`yin/gensym-sym` are built with `symbol`. The `#?(:cljd …)`-first\n   529\tconditional and `#?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default)` catch form\n   530\tare used as elsewhere in v2. Test discovery: confirm \"Testing\n   531\tyin.vm.macro-test\" in the shadow `:node-test` output.\n   532\t\n   533\t---\n   534\t\n   535\t## 7. Phased implementation roadmap\n   536\t\n   537\t### Phase 0 — Spec and codec\n   538\t\n   539\t- This document. `macro-design.md` and `cross-language-macro.md` amended to\n   540\t  point here. The divergence register's macro item becomes: \"user-defined\n   541\t  macros are expanded by a process between media, never by an evaluator;\n   542\t  call sites are ordinary applications; `eval` does not expand.\" Its Macros\n   543\t  section records the deviations of Appendix A.\n   544\t- `yin.vm`: `schema` gains `:yin/root` and `:yin/macro-name`, drops\n   545\t  `:yin/phase-policy`, `:yin/phase`, `:yin/capability`, `:yin/source-call`,\n   546\t  `:yin/macro`, `:yin/expansion-root`, `:yin/error` (event attributes move\n   547\t  to the expander's emitter). `ast->datoms-with-root` emits the root fact;\n   548\t  `index-datoms` honours it (last wins, dangling error); the codec drops the\n   549\t  `:yin/macro-expand` arms and the `:phase-policy` handling.\n   550\t- `ast-walker`: the `:lambda` arm and `datoms->ast` continue to ignore\n   551\t  `:macro?`; nothing else changes. The namespace docstring's \"no macro\n   552\t  branch\" remark becomes the statement of decision 1.\n   553\t- New `test/yin/vm_test.cljc` for the codec: root fact wins; dangling\n   554\t  root errors; heuristic fallback unchanged; `compile` output unchanged for\n   555\t  macro-free programs.\n   556\t\n   557\t### Phase 1 — Expander and yang\n   558\t\n   559\tDeliverables: `src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc` (`expand-batch`, `expand`,\n   560\t`definitions`, `invoke`, `bind-params`, `valid-ast?`, `mark-tail`,\n   561\t`run-bounded`, prelude, `stdlib-forms`, `step`); `yang.clojure` per §6.2.\n   562\t\n   563\tTests (`macro_test.cljc`): `defn` via `stdlib-forms` equals the previously\n   564\tnative lowering; definition consumed and replaced by a name literal; a\n   565\tdefinition in an unexecuted branch is harvested; last definition wins;\n   566\toutermost-first (`->`-style macro); shadowing by `let`, `fn`, and by a\n   567\t`defn`'s own params without any frontend hint; a macro name in operand\n   568\tposition is left as a variable; fixpoint and final scan; depth guard with\n   569\tchain; per-expansion bound from node count before emission; per-batch bound;\n   570\tstep budget on an infinite body; parked body rejected; cyclic input; invalid\n   571\toutput including a host function inside a literal and an `:eid` key; arity\n   572\tand `&`; gensym uniqueness; a stream effect in a body fails with the\n   573\tconstructor error; `mark-tail` on `if`/`let`/lambda spines; determinism\n   574\t(two runs, equal vectors); macro-free batch returned equal modulo the root\n   575\tfact; a corpus-scale batch at 80% of a configured per-batch bound passes;\n   576\tthe forwarder driven through `observer/run-on-stream` including a `full`\n   577\tretry and a malformed-batch retry.\n   578\t\n   579\t### Phase 2 — REPL composition\n   580\t\n   581\tDeliverables per §6.1. Tests: define in one input, use in the next; use\n   582\tfrom `:python` after defining in `:clojure`; `(reset)` forgets; `(compile)`\n   583\trenders four stages; a runtime error from a macro-named variable in operand\n   584\tposition is an unbound-variable error, not a call; the `:semantic` VM type\n   585\truns the same session with equal values.\n   586\t\n   587\t### Phase 3 — Cross-language verification\n   588\t\n   589\tTests (`python_macro_test.clj`, `php_macro_test.clj`): a scalar-signature\n   590\tClojure macro `(defmacro twice [f] (yin/application f [(yin/application f\n   591\t[(yin/literal 1)])]))` called as `twice(inc_fn)` from Python and\n   592\t`twice($inc_fn)` from PHP; `yin/name-of` with a Python string, a Clojure\n   593\tsymbol, and a `:variable`; a PHP `for`-loop variable shadowing a macro name;\n   594\tthe source batch unchanged after expansion; equal values on walker and\n   595\tsemantic. Python `defn` stays reserved until the parser has list literals.\n   596\t\n   597\t### Reserved\n   598\t\n   599\tThe expander as a `yin.vm` program (requires a bounded-child-evaluation\n   600\tprimitive — the general form of `run-bounded`); Shibi at the medium\n   601\tboundary; automatic hygiene; DaoDB-backed store seeding; durable\n   602\tcross-medium provenance as a transactor concern; expansion memo keyed by\n   603\tcall structure (sound because expansion is pure, but each expansion mints\n   604\tits own event and gensyms); Python/PHP `defmacro` surfaces.\n   605\t\n   606\t---\n   607\t\n   608\t## Appendix A. Deviations from prior documents, with reasons\n   609\t\n   610\tFrom `macro-design.md` (v1):\n   611\t\n   612\t1. **AST maps, not eids, as macro arguments.** Every frontend speaks maps;\n   613\t   the datom form is the transport, not the API.\n   614\t2. **Outermost-first, not post-order.** `->`-style macros are impossible\n   615\t   innermost-first, and outermost-first is what makes lexical shadowing\n   616\t   computable without frontend hints.\n   617\t3. **Fixpoint is recursive re-expansion of output**, not \"a pass emits zero\n   618\t   new macro datoms\" — per node rather than per sweep, which is what makes\n   619\t   depth a meaningful guard.\n   620\t4. **No phase policy, no `maybe-recompile-at-boundary`.** Both existed to\n   621\t   describe expansion happening inside an executing VM.\n   622\t\n   623\tFrom `cross-language-macro.md`:\n   624\t\n   625\t5. **Frontends do not discover or coerce.** §3 of that document made the\n   626\t   frontend responsible for knowing which names are macros and for\n   627\t   normalizing arguments; both are the expander's, and a call site is an\n   628\t   ordinary application. Its ordering rule (§4: commit definitions first for\n   629\t   durable ids) is retained.\n   630\t\n   631\tFrom the previous revision of this document — the evaluator-integrated\n   632\tdesign, withdrawn 2026-09-13:\n   633\t\n   634\t6. **No runtime expansion inside evaluators.** That design gave the walker\n   635\t   a `:yin/macro-expand` transition, the semantic VM a `:macro-call`\n   636\t   instruction with ephemeral segments and a retention problem, and the\n   637\t   stack VM `OP_MACRO_EXPAND`. All of it was the cost of running a\n   638\t   syntax-side process inside a semantics-side VM. With expansion on its\n   639\t   own medium the machinery has nowhere to live. What runtime expansion\n   640\t   bought — \"the definition arrived after the program loaded\" — is, in a\n   641\t   stream, \"another batch arrived\".\n   642\t7. **No ledger on the VM, no `:macro-eval` / `:macro-authorize` /\n   643\t   `:capability` options, no `:macro-alloc`, no snapshot fields.** The\n   644\t   expander owns its state and its log writer.\n   645\t8. **No macro flags on closures and no \"macro applied as function\" checks**\n   646\t   at three hot-loop sites (its decision 10). Definitions never reach an\n   647\t   evaluator (decision 5), so there is no closure to flag; a missed macro is\n   648\t   an unbound variable.\n   649\t9. **No `:yin/macro-expand` node type** and no frontend `macro-env`\n   650\t   threading through `yang.clojure`, `yang.python`, `yang.php`, or the\n   651\t   shell. In datoms, operands are already syntax.\n   652\t10. **No shadow hints** (`:yang/shadow-params-operand`,\n   653\t    `:yang/shadow-body-start`, and their proposed promotion to facts).\n   654\t    Outermost-first plus lexical shadow tracking in the expander subsumes\n   655\t    them (§3.2).\n   656\t11. **Self-contained output instead of shared subtrees by reference.** That\n   657\t    design's `:existing` emitter option, structural identity rule,\n   658\t    `:keep-eids?` predicate, and forged-eid check served cross-batch\n   659\t    sharing that is only meaningful for durable ids. Copying is the honest\n   660\t    baseline; sharing returns, if at all, as a transactor concern.\n   661\t12. **Events on a log medium, not interleaved with program.** That design\n   662\t    needed `index-datoms` to exclude event entities from root discovery;\n   663\t    here the evaluator never sees one.\n   664\t13. **Kept from that revision:** the explicit `:yin/root` fact (now a codec\n   665\t    improvement on its own merits); the closed-vocabulary validator with\n   666\t    recursive plain-data checks; dual guards and the fixed traversal order;\n   667\t    `mark-tail` as an expander pass; the fuelled body runner; closed syntax\n   668\t    transformers with no store access; `:yin/macro-name` and logical\n   669\t    `:yin/timestamp`; the loader-adapter (never `comp`) correction to the\n   670\t    semantic spec, which stands independently.\n   671\t\n   672\t## Appendix B. Risks the implementer should watch\n   673\t\n   674\t- Land `:yin/root` in the codec before the expander; until then an expanded\n   675\t  batch's root is found by heuristic and copy order matters.\n   676\t- Definitions must be *replaced*, not merely recorded; a forwarded\n   677\t  `(yin/def defn <lambda>)` would give the evaluator a plain function named\n   678\t  `defn` — the exact footgun decision 5 exists to prevent. The Phase 1 final\n   679\t  scan is the guard.\n   680\t- The shadow set must include the params of a lambda in *operator* position\n   681\t  before descending into its body; `let` and `do` lower to exactly that\n   682\t  shape.\n   683\t- `mark-tail` must recurse through `:if` branches and immediately-applied\n   684\t  lambdas; the semantic-VM tail-loop test is the guard.\n   685\t- `run-bounded` must check `:parked` and `:ready-queue` explicitly:\n   686\t  `:vm/park` sets `:halted? true` and `halted?` does not inspect them.\n   687\t- Do not let `:staged` be dropped on `full`; `run-on-stream` relies on\n   688\t  `ready?` staying false until the append succeeds.\n   689\t- yang deletions in §6.2 remove the native `defn` lowering; every test that\n   690\t  used `defn` without `stdlib-forms` now depends on the expander's seeded\n   691\t  store.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
