{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/stream_observer.cljc | sed -n '172,207p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc | sed -n '80,117p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc | sed -n '327,346p'; sed -n '1,95p' .agents/skills/code-review-pr/references/review_checklist.md\"","aggregated_output":"   172\t   published: the caller retains the previous observer cursor and the same\n   173\t   malformed batch is retried on the next call.\"\n   174\t  [session ready? load-program run-vm]\n   175\t  (loop [{:keys [observer vm]} session]\n   176\t    (if-not (ready? vm)\n   177\t      (let [vm' (run-vm vm)]\n   178\t        (if (ready? vm')\n   179\t          (recur {:observer observer, :vm vm'})\n   180\t          {:observer observer, :vm vm'}))\n   181\t      ;; The load is the step's effect, so the cursor advances only once it has\n   182\t      ;; returned: a throwing loader propagates before any successor exists.\n   183\t      (let [observed (observe/step (:stream observer)\n   184\t                                   (:cursor observer)\n   185\t                                   (fn [batch]\n   186\t                                     {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok,\n   187\t                                      :yin.vm.stream-observer/loaded\n   188\t                                      (load-program vm batch)}))]\n   189\t        (case (:status observed)\n   190\t          :advance (let [vm' (run-vm (:yin.vm.stream-observer/loaded\n   191\t                                       (:effect observed)))\n   192\t                         observer' (assoc observer :cursor (:cursor observed))]\n   193\t                     (if (ready? vm')\n   194\t                       (recur {:observer observer', :vm vm'})\n   195\t                       {:observer observer', :vm vm'}))\n   196\t          :gap (recur {:observer (adopt-gap observer observed), :vm vm})\n   197\t          ;; :retry (blocked) and :ended retain the cursor and end the round.\n   198\t          (:retry :ended) {:observer observer, :vm vm}\n   199\t          (observation-terminal observed observer))))))\n    80\t                   (:call-capacity vm)\n    81\t                   (:ready-queue vm)\n    82\t                   (:store vm)\n    83\t                   val\n    84\t                   (:wait-set vm)\n    85\t                   (:telemetry vm)\n    86\t                   (:telemetry-step vm)\n    87\t                   (:telemetry-t vm)\n    88\t                   (:vm-model vm))))\n    89\t\n    90\t\n    91\t(defn- handle-primitive-result\n    92\t  \"Shared logic for handling the result of a primitive function application.\n    93\t   Handles effect dispatch and blocking via engine/handle-effect.\"\n    94\t  [state result k env]\n    95\t  (if (module/effect? result)\n    96\t    (let [{:keys [state value blocked?]}\n    97\t          (engine/handle-effect\n    98\t            state\n    99\t            result\n   100\t            {:restore-fn ast-walker-restore,\n   101\t             :park-entry-fns {:stream/put (fn [_s _e r]\n   102\t                                            {:k k,\n   103\t                                             :env env,\n   104\t                                             :reason :put,\n   105\t                                             :stream-id (:stream-id r),\n   106\t                                             :datom (:val result)}),\n   107\t                              :stream/next (fn [_s _e r]\n   108\t                                             {:k k,\n   109\t                                              :env env,\n   110\t                                              :reason :next,\n   111\t                                              :cursor-ref (:cursor-ref r),\n   112\t                                              :stream-id (:stream-id r)})}})]\n   113\t      (if blocked?\n   114\t        (assoc state\n   115\t               :control nil\n   116\t               :k nil\n   117\t               :halted? false)\n   327\t\n   328\t\n   329\t(defn run-loop\n   330\t  \"Generic eval loop with scheduler support.\"\n   331\t  [state active? step-fn resume-fn restore-fn]\n   332\t  (loop [v state]\n   333\t    (let [q (or (:ready-queue v) [])]\n   334\t      (cond (active? v) (recur (step-fn v))\n   335\t            (:blocked? v) (let [v' (check-wait-set v restore-fn)]\n   336\t                            (if-let [resumed (or (rt/run-once v')\n   337\t                                                 (resume-fn v'))]\n   338\t                              (recur resumed)\n   339\t                              (telemetry/emit-snapshot v' :blocked)))\n   340\t            (seq q) (if-let [resumed (or (rt/run-once v) (resume-fn v))]\n   341\t                      (recur resumed)\n   342\t                      v)\n   343\t            :else (if (:halted? v) (telemetry/emit-snapshot v :halt) v)))))\n   344\t\n   345\t\n   346\t(defn resume-from-run-queue\n# Code Review PR Checklist & Taxonomy\n\nWhen performing code reviews, evaluate changes using this structured checklist and severity taxonomy.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Finding Severity Taxonomy\n\n| Severity | Label | Definition | Examples |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **P0** | **Critical (Blocker)** | Security vulnerabilities, data loss risks, production crashes, secret leakage. Must be fixed before merging. | Hardcoded tokens, unhandled NullPointer on critical path, SQL injection, broken auth check. |\n| **P1** | **High (Must Address)** | Logic bugs, breaking API changes, missing error handling on external calls, race conditions, lack of tests for complex logic. | Swallowed exceptions, broken retry loop, missing backward compatibility in API schema. |\n| **P2** | **Medium (Recommended)** | Performance inefficiencies, code duplication, sub-optimal data structures, non-idiomatic style. | Unnecessary DB query inside loop, missing index, missing parameter validation. |\n| **P3** | **Low (Nit / Suggestion)** | Minor formatting, naming improvements, leftover comments/TODOs, minor doc polish. | Variable naming clarity, typos in docstrings, cleaning leftover TODO comments. |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Comprehensive Review Checklist\n\n### A. Logic & Correctness\n- [ ] **Edge Cases**: Does the code handle null/nil/undefined, empty collections, zero values, boundary numbers, and unexpected inputs?\n- [ ] **State & Concurrency**: Are shared state mutations thread-safe? Are locks acquired/released properly without deadlock risks?\n- [ ] **Control Flow**: Are all conditional branches covered? Do loops have guaranteed termination conditions?\n- [ ] **Error Propagation**: Are errors caught and logged/surfaced appropriately, rather than swallowed silently (`catch (Exception e) {}`)?\n\n### B. Security & Credentials\n- [ ] **No Hardcoded Secrets**: Are API keys, passwords, bearer tokens, or private keys excluded from code and config?\n- [ ] **Input Validation & Sanitization**: Are external inputs validated before processing or passing to system shells, DB queries, or HTML rendering?\n- [ ] **Authentication & Authorization**: Do endpoints verify user identity and check permissions before executing state changes?\n- [ ] **Safe Dependencies**: Are newly added packages pinned to trusted versions without known vulnerabilities?\n\n### C. Architecture & API Design\n- [ ] **Preserve API Contracts**: Are public method signatures, REST endpoints, and schema definitions backward compatible?\n- [ ] **Single Responsibility**: Are modules, classes, and functions focused on a clear, single responsibility?\n- [ ] **Coupling & Cohesion**: Is the new code properly modularized without introducing circular dependencies or leaky abstractions?\n- [ ] **Idempotence**: Are retry loops or state-changing operations safe to re-execute on transient failures?\n\n### D. Performance & Resource Efficiency\n- [ ] **N+1 Queries**: Are database or remote API calls executed in batches rather than inside loops?\n- [ ] **Resource Cleanup**: Are file handles, database connections, streams, and web sockets closed explicitly (or managed via try-with-resources / context managers)?\n- [ ] **Memory & Allocation**: Are large datasets streamed or paginated rather than loaded fully into memory at once?\n\n### E. Test Coverage & Quality\n- [ ] **Test Coverage**: Do new features or bug fixes include corresponding unit or integration tests?\n- [ ] **Test Quality**: Do tests verify actual assertions rather than just checking that code runs without throwing?\n- [ ] **Edge Case Tests**: Are failure modes, invalid inputs, and error handlers covered by unit tests?\n\n---\n\n## 3. Review Feedback Guidelines\n\n1. **Be Specific**: Always reference the exact file line number, function name, and context.\n2. **Explain the \"Why\"**: Don't just say \"change this\". Explain the risk or benefit (e.g., \"This could lead to a race condition if two threads call `transact!` simultaneously...\").\n3. **Provide Concrete Fixes**: Offer concrete, copy-pasteable diff snippets where appropriate.\n4. **Acknowledge Good Work**: Highlight well-crafted solutions, thorough tests, or clean refactorings.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
